.Dd August 7, 2026
.Dt XTC_DISPATCH 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_dispatch ,
.Nm xtc_dispatch_cancel ,
.Nm xtc_dispatch_handle_free
.Nd submit an effect to the runtime from a callback or a foreign thread
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_dispatch.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_dispatch "xtc_loop_t *loop" "int (*fn)(void *)" "void *arg" "xtc_future_t **out_future" "xtc_dispatch_handle_t **out_handle"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_dispatch_cancel "xtc_dispatch_handle_t *h"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_dispatch_handle_free "xtc_dispatch_handle_t *h"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The dispatcher is the blessed one-call front door for crossing
.Em into
the runtime from an impure callback or a foreign OS thread -- a C
library's completion callback, a signal handler's follow-up, an
embedder's own I/O thread -- with a way to await the effect's result
and to cancel it.  It is the inverse of
.Xr xtc_blocking 3 ,
which runs blocking work
.Em off
a loop: the dispatcher gets work
.Em onto
a loop from the outside.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_dispatch
spawns a fiber on
.Fa loop
that runs
.Fa fn Ns ( Ns Fa arg Ns )
to completion, then completes a future with
.Fa fn Ns 's
return value.  On
.Dv XTC_OK :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fa out_future
receives a future that resolves with
.Fa fn Ns 's
return value (the value is
.Fa "(intptr_t)fn(arg)" ,
status
.Dv XTC_OK )
when
.Fa fn
returns, or with (value 0, status
.Dv XTC_E_ABORTED )
if the dispatch is cancelled or the fiber crashes first.  The caller
owns and consumes it with
.Xr xtc_future_await 3
(from a fiber) or
.Xr xtc_future_wait 3
(from a plain OS thread), exactly like any other future.  This
argument is required.
.It Fa out_handle
receives an opaque cancel handle, or may be
.Dv NULL
if the caller never needs to cancel (fire and await).  When non-NULL
the caller must eventually pass the handle to
.Fn xtc_dispatch_cancel
or
.Fn xtc_dispatch_handle_free
to release it.
.El
.Pp
.Fn xtc_dispatch_cancel
requests cancellation of the dispatched fiber and releases the handle.
Cancellation is cooperative and composes with the runtime's masking and
scope machinery: the dispatched fiber observes it at its next
cancellation point -- a park,
.Xr xtc_cancel_requested 3 ,
or the end of an
.Xr xtc_uncancelable 3
region -- runs any
.Xr xtc_scope 3
or at-exit finalizers (so a resource acquired under a scope is still
released), and unwinds.  If the fiber has already completed this is a
harmless no-op on the result; either way the future is guaranteed to
resolve exactly once.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_dispatch_handle_free
releases a cancel handle without cancelling, for the caller that
decided it will never cancel this dispatch.
.Ss Thread-safety
.Fn xtc_dispatch
and
.Fn xtc_dispatch_cancel
are safe to call from
.Em any
OS thread, including one libxtc does not manage.  This is not new
machinery: the dispatcher inherits the thread-safety of
.Xr xtc_proc_spawn 3
(a cross-thread spawn posts to the target loop's MPSC inbox and pings
its I/O backend) and of
.Xr xtc_promise_set 3
(documented safe from any thread), composed with the masking and scope
core from
.Xr xtc_uncancelable 3
and
.Xr xtc_scope 3 .
.Ss Exactly-once resolution
The future always resolves exactly once: with
.Fa fn Ns 's
value on a normal return, or with
.Dv XTC_E_ABORTED
if the fiber is cancelled or crashes before it returns -- never lost,
never doubled, never a hang.  An awaiter therefore never blocks
forever on a dispatch that was cancelled or that faulted.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_dispatch
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
on success (with
.Fa *out_future
and, if requested,
.Fa *out_handle
written),
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a
.Dv NULL
.Fa loop ,
.Fa fn ,
or
.Fa out_future ,
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
on allocation failure, or a spawn error such as
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE
if the loop's proc or task caps are hit.  On any error nothing is
spawned.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_dispatch_cancel
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
on a
.Dv NULL
handle.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A foreign thread submits an effect and waits for its result:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int
compute(void *arg)
{
	return (int)(intptr_t)arg * 2;
}

/* On a thread that is NOT a libxtc carrier: */
xtc_future_t *fut;
intptr_t out;

if (xtc_dispatch(loop, compute, (void *)(intptr_t)21,
    &fut, NULL) == XTC_OK) {
	(void)xtc_future_wait(fut, &out, -1);
	/* out == 42 */
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_blocking 3 ,
.Xr xtc_future 3 ,
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_scope 3 ,
.Xr xtc_uncancelable 3
